When you see that Spirit 0’s position changes much more than the other robots—even though the forces are theoretically the same—it usually means that subtle differences in the constraint setup, mass distribution, and docking geometry are causing an uneven distribution of motion. Let’s break this down in detail:

1. Constraint Chain and Docking Order
	•	Rigid Docking via Trusses:
In your simulation, the three Spirit robots are “docked” together by their trusses. This docking creates a closed-loop chain of constraints: Spirit 0 is connected to Spirit 1, Spirit 1 to Spirit 2, and Spirit 2 back to Spirit 0. In an ideal, perfectly symmetric system, a change in one truss length might be distributed evenly across all robots. However, even a small asymmetry in the docking geometry can lead to one member (often the one at the “start” of the chain) compensating for most of the length change.
	•	Order of Docking and Numerical Solver:
The order in which the docking constraints are set up (or even the order in which they are processed by the solver) can create a “chain effect.” In many multibody systems, the robot that is the first in the loop may have a bit more freedom to move because the numerical solver distributes errors unevenly. In your case, Spirit 0 ends up taking on more of the positional change needed to satisfy the sinusoidal variation in truss lengths, while Spirit 1 and Spirit 2 are relatively “pinned” by the two constraints they share.

2. Differences in Mass, Inertia, and Friction
	•	Small Differences Amplify Over Time:
Even if the robots are designed to be identical, manufacturing or numerical differences (even at the level of floating-point roundoff errors) can lead to small differences in mass or inertia. The Spirit that has a slightly lower effective inertia in the direction of the force will tend to accelerate more.
	•	Friction and Terrain Interaction:
The interaction with the terrain might not be identical for all three. If Spirit 0 has a slightly lower frictional grip or if its contact points with the terrain differ, it might be easier for it to move in response to the changing constraints.

3. Distribution of Sinusoidal Forces
	•	Sinusoidal Variation and Phase Offsets:
The truss lengths are updated sinusoidally with phase differences. While the intended idea is that each docking link experiences a similar magnitude of force, the phase offsets (0, PI/3, 2*PI/3) mean that at any given moment, the desired length change is different for each connection. If one connection is at the peak of its expansion or contraction, that robot may experience a higher corrective force.
	•	Force Balance in a Closed Loop:
In a closed chain, the forces must balance out overall. If one member (Spirit 0) is more “free” to adjust its position, then it can absorb most of the corrective motion while the other robots—being more tightly constrained by two connections each—remain more stationary.

4. Solver and Dynamic Effects
	•	Iterative Solvers and Constraint Drift:
The Chrono solver iteratively resolves the forces and constraints at each timestep. Small errors or asymmetries in constraint enforcement can lead the solver to adjust one robot’s position more than the others to maintain overall equilibrium.
	•	Degrees of Freedom:
Even though each connection is meant to control the distance between two bodies, the constraints do not fully lock all degrees of freedom (for example, the robots are free to rotate or shift laterally if it helps reduce constraint violations). Spirit 0 might be in a configuration where the residual degrees of freedom allow it to move more easily in order to satisfy the sinusoidal length changes.

Summary

In your simulation, the most likely cause is that the docking order and the way the trusses (arms) are connected in a closed loop create a situation where Spirit 0 is the “weak link” or the one with the most freedom to compensate for the sinusoidal length changes. Although the applied forces are similar in magnitude, even very small asymmetries (in docking positions, mass/inertia, terrain contact, or solver numerical errors) can cause Spirit 0 to absorb most of the movement, while Spirits 1 and 2 remain relatively fixed.

